---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Answer from "../components/Answer.astro";
import { loadMeta } from "../lib/catalog.js";
import { EIA_ELECTRICITY_SOURCE, EIA_GASOLINE_SOURCE, EIA_LICENSE, loadPriceSnapshot } from "../lib/eia-prices.js";
import { COMPONENT_MIN } from "../lib/components.js";
import { loadInvestigationPages } from "../lib/investigations.js";
import { HYBRID_MIN_YEAR, HYBRID_PAIR_LIMIT } from "../lib/hybrid-pairs.js";
import { formatPeriod } from "../lib/fuel-cost.js";
import { formatDate, formatNumber, metaDescription } from "../lib/format.js";
import { dataset } from "../lib/schema.js";
import { site } from "../lib/site.js";

const meta = loadMeta();
const counts = meta.counts;
const dates = meta.dates;
const eiaPrices = loadPriceSnapshot(readFileSync(new URL("../../data/eia-fuel-prices.csv", import.meta.url), "utf8"));
const eiaGas = eiaPrices.find((row) => row.product === "gasoline" && row.area === "US");
const eiaElectric = eiaPrices.find((row) => row.product === "electricity" && row.area === "US");
const investigationPages = loadInvestigationPages();
const text = `Counts on this site come from NHTSA flat files dated ${formatDate(dates.recalls)} (recalls), ${formatDate(dates.complaints)} (complaints), and ${formatDate(dates.investigations)} (investigations), and from the EPA fuel-economy file dated ${formatDate(dates.epa)}. Complaint totals are distinct complaint numbers, not raw rows.`;
const path = "/methodology/";
---

<Base
  title="Methodology"
  description={metaDescription(text)}
  canonicalPath={path}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Methodology", href: path }]}
  jsonLd={[
    dataset({
      name: "VehicleFactbook methodology and source files",
      description: "How VehicleFactbook counts NHTSA recalls, complaints, and investigations and how it joins them to EPA model years.",
      url: new URL(path, site.url).href,
      dateModified: dates.complaints,
    }),
  ]}
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>How the files are counted</h1>
    <Answer text={text} />

    <h2>Which vehicles get a page</h2>
    <p>
      The spine is the EPA fueleconomy.gov vehicles file, model year 2000 and newer: {formatNumber(counts.epaRowsMy2000)} rows, {formatNumber(counts.rawMakes)} case-sensitive make names, {formatNumber(counts.rawModels)} case-sensitive models, and {formatNumber(counts.mmySpine)} make, model, and model year combinations. File date {formatDate(dates.epa)}.
    </p>
    <p>
      Spellings that normalize to the same name share a page. That is why the site publishes {formatNumber(counts.publishedMakes)} makes and {formatNumber(counts.publishedModels)} models. Mini and MINI are one make page. Nine model spellings fold the same way, including Nissan LEAF and Leaf, and Chevrolet TrailBlazer and Trailblazer. NHTSA's files also contain motorcycles, trailers, and equipment. Those are not given pages, because they are not in the EPA passenger-vehicle list.
    </p>

    <h2>Names do not match across agencies</h2>
    <p>
      EPA and NHTSA spell models differently. A model year is matched only when a rule or a reviewed row points at the same vehicle. Punctuation and case are removed first. An exact name wins. A reviewed row in <span class="mono">data/model-crosswalk.csv</span> wins over a weaker guess, and it counts only in model years where that NHTSA name is actually present. Class names such as C-Class and series names such as 3 Series follow. A pickup, cab, or body suffix is taken off the EPA name only when what remains equals one NHTSA name, and not when another EPA model that year starts with the same stem. Cargo and passenger splits are not folded together. A slash in an EPA base model, such as Golf/GTI, can match each part that is itself an NHTSA name. A prefix matches only when the next character is a letter, so CX-5 does not absorb CX-50 and Q5 does not absorb Q50. If two EPA models claim one NHTSA name equally, neither gets it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Of {formatNumber(counts.mmySpine)} model years, {formatNumber(counts.matchedMmy)} match at least one NHTSA name and {formatNumber(counts.unmatchedMmy)} do not. An unmatched page still shows EPA fuel economy and says that no NHTSA name was matched. It does not borrow another vehicle's complaints.
    </p>
    <p>
      When the EPA name itself is one of the matched NHTSA names, the headline counts are that name only. Other matched names, such as a hybrid, are listed separately and are not added in. When the EPA name is a class or series and no exact name exists, the headline is the sum of the matched names and the page says so. {formatNumber(counts.yearsPages)} models have a by-year page because at least three model years have a matched recall, complaint, or investigation.
    </p>
    <p>Match methods for the {formatNumber(counts.matchedMmy)} matched model years:</p>
    <ul>
      {
        Object.entries(counts.matchMethods).map(([method, count]) => (
          <li>
            {method}: {formatNumber(count)}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2>Complaints</h2>
    <p>
      The complaint file dated {formatDate(dates.complaints)} has {formatNumber(counts.complaintRows)} rows and {formatNumber(counts.complaintVehicleRows)} vehicle rows. A complaint is repeated once per component, so the published count is distinct ODINO values: {formatNumber(counts.complaintVehicleOdino)} vehicle complaints in the whole file. The same rule is used on each model year page.
    </p>
    <p>
      Crash and fire flags are yes if any row for that complaint says yes. The injury and death fields use the highest number on that complaint, then add those highs. A complaint can appear in more than one component, so component bars can add up to more than the complaint total. The top ten components are shown. A name in that list with at least {COMPONENT_MIN} distinct complaints links to a count page.
    </p>
    <p>
      Names, partial VINs, cities, dealers, phone numbers, and the narrative text are not stored and are not published. Newer model years have had less time to accumulate complaints. The file's own documentation describes a format change in 2021. A low count on a new model year is "complaints received so far," dated with the file.
    </p>

    <h2>Recalls</h2>
    <p>
      Both NHTSA recall files are imported. The post-2010 file dated {formatDate(dates.recalls)} has {formatNumber(counts.recallRowsPost)} rows and {formatNumber(counts.vehicleCampaignsPost2010)} vehicle campaigns. The pre-2010 file has {formatNumber(counts.recallRowsPre)} rows and {formatNumber(counts.vehicleCampaignsPre2010)} vehicle campaigns, so model years 2000–2009 are not limited to the later file. Five short pre-2010 rows that do not have the full field list are skipped.
    </p>
    <p>
      A model year page lists each campaign once. The potential-affected number on a campaign is a campaign-level figure, so it is not printed on a model year page where it could be read as a count for that model. Do-not-drive and park-outside badges are the file's Yes/No flags. The lists at <a href="/recalls/do-not-drive/">do not drive</a> ({formatNumber(counts.doNotDrive)} vehicle campaigns) and <a href="/recalls/park-outside/">park outside</a> ({formatNumber(counts.parkOutside)}) use those flags.
    </p>
    <p>
      A vehicle campaign whose number is from 2015 or later, and that is listed on at least one model year page, has its own page at <span class="mono">/recalls/</span> plus the campaign number. That page repeats the component, defect summary, report date, and flags stored on those rows, and it links back to each model year and to the make page. A campaign that no model year lists is not given a page. Campaign numbers from 2014 and earlier still link to NHTSA.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/recalls/takata-airbag/">Takata list</a> ({formatNumber(counts.takata)} vehicle campaigns) is every vehicle campaign whose component, defect, consequence, or notes field contains the word Takata. Campaigns that do not use that word are missing from the list. The <a href="/recalls/this-week/">this week</a> page is the seven days ending on the newest report date in the file, not the calendar week when the site was built.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no expiration column. Whether a free repair still applies to one vehicle is a VIN question, and this site links to NHTSA's tool instead of answering it.
    </p>

    <h2>Investigations</h2>
    <p>
      The investigations file dated {formatDate(dates.investigations)} has {formatNumber(counts.investigationRows)} rows and {formatNumber(counts.investigationActions)} actions. {formatNumber(counts.investigationActionsNoCloseDate)} actions have no close date on any row. The page says "no close date in the NHTSA file." It does not say the investigation is still open. The investigations dictionary file is older than the data file. Subject lines are shortened. The long summary text is not copied onto the page. Action numbers are shown as text because this site does not have a verified public URL for each action.
    </p>
    <p>
      An action attached to a published model year, with no close date on any stored row, has its own page under <a href="/investigations/">/investigations/</a>. That set is {formatNumber(investigationPages.length)} actions. The file count above also includes actions that are not attached to a published model year, and those actions do not have a page. The model year table links the action number when a page exists. The investigation page lists the shortened subject, the component, the open date, and the model years. It does not copy the summary.
    </p>

    <h2>Fuel economy</h2>
    <p>
      Each EPA row is one configuration. Combined city and highway figures of zero are left blank. feScore and ghgScore values of −1 are left blank and are not shown as a score. fuelCost08 is EPA's annual fuel cost for that configuration. EPA's documentation says the figure uses 15,000 miles and 55 percent city driving. The calculator still shows that published figure and does not replace it. Electric vehicles report MPGe in the combined field and kWh per 100 miles separately. A combined figure for an electric configuration is not labeled mpg.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fuel-cost calculator also multiplies the miles you enter by the EPA rate and by a price. For regular gasoline and diesel, the price box can start from the weekly retail average, including taxes, in EIA's <a href={EIA_GASOLINE_SOURCE}>Gasoline and Diesel Fuel Update</a>. For electricity, it can start from the residential price in <a href={EIA_ELECTRICITY_SOURCE}>Electric Power Monthly Table 5.6.A</a>. The committed snapshot is data/eia-fuel-prices.csv, retrieved {formatDate(eiaGas.retrieved)}. Gasoline and diesel use the week of {formatDate(eiaGas.period)}. Electricity uses {formatPeriod(eiaElectric.period)}, and EIA marks those 2026 values preliminary. Premium gasoline, midgrade gasoline, and other fuels have no default. Clearing the price leaves the calculated cost blank and leaves fuelCost08 as published. <a href={EIA_LICENSE}>EIA's copyright and reuse note</a> places U.S. government publications on its site in the public domain.
    </p>

    <h2>Star ratings</h2>
    <p>
      Stars come from NHTSA's Safety Ratings API for model years 2011 and newer. Older stars are a pre-2011 rating, not comparable, and are not shown. "Not rated" stays as text. Rollover possibility is printed as the API sends it, including values between 0 and 1, and is not turned into a percent. Crash-test photos link to static.nhtsa.gov and are not embedded. If a model year in the API could not be fetched, the page says no rating was matched rather than filling a number.
    </p>
    {
      Object.keys(meta.ncap || {}).length ? (
        <ul>
          {Object.entries(meta.ncap).map(([year, info]) => (
            <li>
              Model year {year}: {formatNumber(info.variants)} variants, {formatNumber(info.failures)} fetch failures.
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      ) : (
        <p>No Safety Ratings cache was present when this copy of the site was built.</p>
      )
    }

    <h2>Example comparisons</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compare/">Compare</a> publishes static pages for pairs of models from different makes.
      A pair uses the newest model year for which both models have an NHTSA name match.
      That year has to share an EPA vehicle class, the makes have to share a tier, and the model year has to share a fuel type.
      Tiers are volume, luxury, and specialty. Fuel types are gasoline (regular, midgrade, premium, and flex-fuel), diesel, electric, hydrogen, and natural gas.
      Drive-type labels are removed before the class is compared.
      The page shows EPA fuel figures, NHTSA overall stars for model year 2011 and newer, recall campaign counts, and complaint counts received so far.
      The set is drawn from EPA configuration counts in the committed files. It is not a sales ranking.
      An older example address that this rule drops stays on the site as a noindex refresh to a replacement page, and that address is left out of the sitemap.
    </p>
    <h2>Hybrid and gasoline</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compare/hybrid/">Hybrid vs gasoline</a> compares conventional gasoline configurations with hybrid or plug-in hybrid configurations of the same make and base model.
      A sibling EPA name that adds Hybrid, PHEV, Prime, or Energi stays with that base model.
      The model year is the newest one in which the EPA file lists both, from model year {HYBRID_MIN_YEAR} on.
      The hybrid or plug-in hybrid side's highest combined mpg is at least as high as the gasoline side's highest combined mpg.
      Up to {HYBRID_PAIR_LIMIT} pairs are published, ordered by EPA configuration rows. That order is not a sales ranking.
      City, highway, and combined mpg and EPA's annual fuel cost (fuelCost08) are stated side by side.
      When every configuration on both sides uses regular gasoline or diesel, the page also shows a cost calculated at 15,000 miles from EPA combined mpg and the committed EIA price snapshot.
      That calculated cost does not replace fuelCost08.
      Complaint counts, when a model year has an NHTSA name match, are the counts for that model year. They are not split by configuration.
    </p>

    <h2>Component pages</h2>
    <p>
      A component page is published at <span class="mono">/make/model/year/component/</span> when the component list on that model year includes the component with at least {COMPONENT_MIN} distinct complaints.
      The list is the top ten components stored for the headline NHTSA name.
      When the EPA name is not itself an NHTSA name, the list is the top ten for the matched name with the most complaints, which is the list the model year page already shows.
      Other matched names stay on the model year page.
      A component outside that stored top ten has no count in the committed file, so it does not get a page.
      The page states the distinct complaint count and the complaint file date.
      It does not state a rate.
      Crash, fire, injury, and death figures stay on the model year page, because the committed file stores those figures for the model year rather than for each component.
      Narratives stay on NHTSA.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site does not say</h2>
    <p>
      Complaint and recall counts are not sales-adjusted. There is no registration denominator in these files. The site does not label a vehicle or a model year as one to avoid, and it does not call one reliable, best, or worst. A higher complaint count can mean more vehicles were sold.
    </p>

    <h2>Not in this version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Manufacturer communications (technical service bulletins). NHTSA's TSBS dictionary says the files were renamed and split into five-year downloads. The zip names tried for this build were not verified, so nothing from those files is published.</li>
      <li>A VIN check. Use <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls">NHTSA's recall tool</a>.</li>
      <li>Campaign pages are only the 2015-or-later vehicle campaigns listed on a model year page.</li>
      <li>The {formatNumber(counts.unmatchedMmy)} unmatched model years have no NHTSA row this site will attach, or the name is ambiguous between two EPA models. Their pages exist, show EPA fuel economy, and say the name did not match.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Source files are U.S. government works. The license reference used in the site's dataset markup is <a href="https://www.usa.gov/government-works">usa.gov/government-works</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
